#rules for naming identifiers in python=>
#1. idenifiers canbe a combination of uppercase and lowercase letters, digits and underscore(..).
#2. An identifiers can not start with a digit. so while variable_1 is valid but 1_variable is not valid.
#3. we can not use special symbols like @, #, $, %, etc in identifiers.
#4. we can not use reserved keywords as identifiers. for example, if, else, while, for, etc are reserved keywords in python and can not be used as variable names.
#5. identifiers can be of any length.


#data types in python=>
#1. int - integer data type, used to represent whole numbers without a fractional part. for example, 0, 1, -5, 100, etc.
#2. float - floating point data type, used to represent numbers with a fractional part. for example, 3.14, -0.5, 2.0, etc.
#3. str - string data type, used to represent a sequence of characters. for example "hello", 'world', "python", etc.
#4. bool - boolean data type, used to represent truth values. it can have only two values: True and False.      
#5. list - list data type, used to represent a collection of items. it is ordered and mutable. for example, [1, 2, 3], ["apple", "banana", "cherry"], etc.
#6. tuple - tuple data type, used to represent a collection of items. it is ordered and immutable. for example, (1, 2, 3), ("apple", "banana", "cherry"), etc.
#7. dict - dictionary data type, used to represent a collection of key-value pairs. it is unordered and mutable. for example, {"name": "dhairya", "age": 16, "price": 25.99}, etc.
#8. set - set data type, used to represent a collection of unique items. it is unordered and mutable. for example, {1, 2, 3}, {"apple", "banana", "cherry"}, etc.
#9. None - None data type, used to represent the absence of a value. it is a special constant in python. for example, None, etc.

